  "query_preview": "../Monday through Wednesday/...Minimal changes were \nmade to the long term forecast as mild weather continues to end \nout the month of October. High pressure remains over the gulf \nthrough the long term period, with brief interruptions by weak low\npressure systems. The first of these will move across the gulf \nMonday bringing increased cloud cover the the chance for light \nprecipitation. The main change with this weak front is that the \ntiming looks to have slightly changed with it arriving slightly \nlater than initially anticipated. Now arriving Sunday night into \nMonday. Currently no impacts are anticipated with just light \nprecipitation expected. The other main change to the long term \nforecast was increasing winds Tuesday as the northerly winds are \nonce again likely to increase as the pressure gradient builds. \nCurrently strongest winds are most likely to occur over Lynn Canal\ninto Skagway. \n\nThere is a chance for some northern areas to see slightly below \naverage temperatures into next week into the high 20s / low 30s. \nThis will be mainly dependent on how clear we get at night, with \nlow cloud cover. Otherwise temperatures remain around typical for \nthis time of year with maximum temperatures in the 40s and low \ntemperatures in the low to mid 30s.",
